DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  kkccmmpp()  system call can be used to check whether the two processes
       identified by _p_i_d_1 and _p_i_d_2 share a kernel  resource  such  as  virtual
       memory, file descriptors, and so on.

       Permission   to  employ  kkccmmpp()  is  governed  by  ptrace  access  mode
       PPTTRRAACCEE__MMOODDEE__RREEAADD__RREEAALLCCRREEDDSS checks  against  both  _p_i_d_1  and  _p_i_d_2;  see
       ppttrraaccee(2).

       The _t_y_p_e argument specifies which resource is to be compared in the two
       processes.  It has one of the following values:

       KKCCMMPP__FFIILLEE
              Check  whether a file descriptor _i_d_x_1 in the process _p_i_d_1 refers
              to the same open file description (see ooppeenn(2)) as file descrip‐
              tor _i_d_x_2 in the process _p_i_d_2.  The existence  of  two  file  de‐
              scriptors that refer to the same open file description can occur
              as a result of dduupp(2) (and similar) ffoorrkk(2), or passing file de‐
              scriptors via a domain socket (see uunniixx(7)).

       KKCCMMPP__FFIILLEESS
              Check  whether the processes share the same set of open file de‐
              scriptors.  The arguments _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are  ignored.   See  the
              discussion of the CCLLOONNEE__FFIILLEESS flag in cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__FFSS
              Check  whether  the processes share the same filesystem informa‐
              tion (i.e., file mode  creation  mask,  working  directory,  and
              filesystem root).  The arguments _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are ignored.  See
              the discussion of the CCLLOONNEE__FFSS flag in cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__IIOO
              Check  whether  the  processes share I/O context.  The arguments
              _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are ignored.  See the discussion of  the  CCLLOONNEE__IIOO
              flag in cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__SSIIGGHHAANNDD
              Check  whether the processes share the same table of signal dis‐
              positions.  The arguments _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are  ignored.   See  the
              discussion of the CCLLOONNEE__SSIIGGHHAANNDD flag in cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__SSYYSSVVSSEEMM
              Check whether the processes share the same list of System V sem‐
              aphore  undo  operations.   The  arguments _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are ig‐
              nored.   See  the  discussion  of  the  CCLLOONNEE__SSYYSSVVSSEEMM  flag   in
              cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__VVMM
              Check  whether  the processes share the same address space.  The
              arguments _i_d_x_1 and _i_d_x_2 are ignored.  See the discussion of  the
              CCLLOONNEE__VVMM flag in cclloonnee(2).

       KKCCMMPP__EEPPOOLLLL__TTFFDD (since Linux 4.13)
              Check  whether  the  file descriptor _i_d_x_1 of the process _p_i_d_1 is
              present in the  eeppoollll(7)  instance  described  by  _i_d_x_2  of  the
              process  _p_i_d_2.   The  argument  _i_d_x_2 is a pointer to a structure
              where the target file is  described.   This  structure  has  the
              form:

           struct kcmp_epoll_slot {
               __u32 efd;
               __u32 tfd;
               __u64 toff;
           };

       Within  this  structure,  _e_f_d is an epoll file descriptor returned from
       eeppoollll__ccrreeaattee(2), _t_f_d is a target file descriptor number, and _t_o_f_f is  a
       target file offset counted from zero.  Several different targets may be
       registered  with the same file descriptor number and setting a specific
       offset helps to investigate each of them.

       Note the kkccmmpp() is not protected against false positives which may  oc‐
       cur  if  the  processes  are  currently  running.   One should stop the
       processes by sending SSIIGGSSTTOOPP (see ssiiggnnaall(7)) prior to  inspection  with
       this system call to obtain meaningful results.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The return value of a successful call to kkccmmpp() is simply the result of
       arithmetic  comparison of kernel pointers (when the kernel compares re‐
       sources, it uses their memory addresses).

       The easiest way to explain is to consider an example.  Suppose that  _v_1
       and  _v_2  are  the  addresses  of appropriate resources, then the return
       value is one of the following:

              00      _v_1 is equal to _v_2; in  other  words,  the  two  processes
                     share the resource.

              11      _v_1 is less than _v_2.

              22      _v_1 is greater than _v_2.

              33      _v_1  is  not  equal to _v_2, but ordering information is un‐
                     available.

       On error, -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

       kkccmmpp() was designed to return values suitable  for  sorting.   This  is
       particularly  handy  if one needs to compare a large number of file de‐
       scriptors.

